{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And I will bring you, etc.</b> It is difficult, for the words of El Elyon are pure — for those who came out of Egypt are the ones who enter the land, as it is written: ״וְהֵבֵאתִי אֶתְכֶם אֶל הָאָרֶץ,״ yet we do not find that this was so; rather, it was their children whom He brought there, whereas the entire generation of those who came out of Egypt, from twenty years old and up, fell — their corpses fell in the wilderness. And the difficulty grows even greater in light of what we have said, that HaShem swore upon all these matters in saying ״לָכֵן,״ and they of blessed memory said (Shemot Rabbah 6:4): ״לָכֵן״ is nothing but an expression of an oath." "Now it appears that the source of wisdom was cunning in resolving this difficulty, for before saying <b>and I will bring you</b> etc., He said <b>and you shall know that I am YHWH who brings out</b> etc. — the meaning is that this is a condition, and upon this condition ״וְהֵבֵאתִי״ etc., and otherwise, if you defy HaShem, this promise does not apply. Therefore He wrote this particular detail of knowing HaShem etc. in the middle of the good promises — neither delaying it to after nor advancing it to before — to say: up to this point it is under oath unconditionally; but this particular detail of bringing them into the land is a conditional matter — ״וִידַעְתֶּם״ etc., then ״הֵבֵאתִי״; and otherwise, whatever will be, will be."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:08.245944398Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}